    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Clarify the requirement.\n\n**What is a design-system architecture?** should define stable foundations, component contracts, accessibility rules, release/versioning policy, and tooling that prevents visual and API drift.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method:\nStart with users, functional requirements, non-functional requirements, scale assumptions, state/data flow, and failure modes. Choose the smallest architecture that satisfies the constraints.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\nThe manual example exposes the core decision, state transition, lookup, or contract so you can explain the reasoning line by line in a system-design interview.\n\n```ts\ninterface Token {\n  name: string;\n  value: string;\n}\n\nfunction resolveToken(\n  tokens: readonly Token[],\n  name: string,\n): string | null {\n  for (let i = 0; i < tokens.length; i += 1) {\n    if (tokens[i].name === name) {\n      return tokens[i].value;\n    }\n  }\n\n  return null;\n}\n\nconst value = resolveToken(\n  [{ name: \"color.text\", value: \"#111\" }],\n  \"color.text\",\n);\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / practical TypeScript:\nUse native collections or platform helpers when they communicate the same architecture clearly and do not hide an important trade-off.\n\n```ts\nconst tokens = new Map([\n  [\"color.text\", \"#111\"],\n]);\n\nconst value =\n  tokens.get(\"color.text\") ?? null;\n```\n\nStep 5 — Production example:\nUse **What is a design-system architecture?** as a design-system contract with a single source of truth, versioning, accessibility, responsive behavior, and regression tests.\n\nStep 6 — Complexity and scale:\nExplain the dominant read/write/render/network cost, the expected growth path, and where caching, partitioning, batching, pagination, or independent deployment would become useful.\n\nStep 7 — Edge cases:\nHandle empty data, partial failure, retries, stale state, unauthorized access, slow networks, large datasets, migration constraints, and rollback where relevant.\n\nStep 8 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain the boundary, the source of truth, the failure/recovery path, the observability signal, and why this design is simpler or safer than the alternatives.",
